window.INSULA_SEARCH=[{"t":"Brain tumours","u":"conditions/brain-tumours.html","k":"Condition","s":"An abnormal growth of cells in or around the brain. Many are not cancer.","b":"A brain tumour is a growth of abnormal cells in the brain or the tissues around it. The word sounds frightening, but brain tumours vary enormously, and many are slow-growing and not cancer.What it is Brain tumours are usually described in two ways. Benign or malignant. Benign tumours are not cancer and tend to grow slowly. A common example is a meningioma , which grows from the brain's outer covering. Malignant tumours are cancer and can grow faster. Primary or secondary. A primary tumour starts in the brain. A secondary tumour (also called a metastasis ) has spread to the brain from cancer elsewhere in the body. Even a benign tumour can cause problems, because the skull is a closed space and a growth can press on nearby brain. Common symptoms Symptoms depend mostly on where the tumour is, not how big it is. They can include: Headaches that are new, keep getting worse, or are worse in the morning or with vomiting A seizure (fit) Weakness or numbness on one side of the body Changes in vision, speech, memory, behaviour or personality Problems with balance or coordination Each of these symptoms has many possible causes, and most are not caused by a tumour. Most headaches are not serious, and a first seizure has many possible causes. But a new pattern of headache, or any of these symptoms that persists or worsens, deserves a medical check. How it is diagnosed An MRI scan with contr"},{"t":"Brain aneurysm","u":"conditions/brain-aneurysm.html","k":"Condition","s":"A small bulge in a weak spot of a blood vessel in the brain. Most never burst.","b":"A brain aneurysm is a bulge in a weak part of a blood vessel wall, a little like a weak spot on a tyre. Most aneurysms never burst, and many are found by chance on a scan done for another reason.What it is Blood vessels in the brain are under constant pressure. Where the wall is weaker, it can slowly balloon outward. This bulge is an aneurysm. If an aneurysm bursts ( ruptures ), blood leaks into the space around the brain. This is called a subarachnoid haemorrhage and is a medical emergency. Symptoms An aneurysm that has not burst usually causes no symptoms at all. Occasionally a larger one presses on a nerve, which can cause a drooping eyelid, double vision, or pain above or behind one eye. A burst aneurysm typically causes a sudden, extremely severe headache, often described as the worst headache of one's life, reaching full strength within seconds. It may come with vomiting, a stiff neck, sensitivity to light, confusion or collapse. Who is more at risk Smoking High blood pressure A close family member who has had an aneurysm Some inherited conditions, such as polycystic kidney disease Stopping smoking and controlling blood pressure are the most useful things most people can do. How it is diagnosed Aneurysms are found with scans that show blood vessels: CT angiography , MR angiography , or a catheter angiogram , where dye is injected through a thin tube passed from the wrist "},{"t":"Hydrocephalus","u":"conditions/hydrocephalus.html","k":"Condition","s":"A build-up of fluid inside the brain that raises pressure. It can affect any age.","b":"Hydrocephalus means a build-up of fluid inside the brain. The brain normally makes and drains a clear fluid all the time. When drainage is blocked or slowed, fluid collects and pressure rises.What it is The fluid is called cerebrospinal fluid , or CSF. It cushions the brain and spinal cord and flows through spaces inside the brain called ventricles . Hydrocephalus can be present from birth, or develop later after a brain bleed, infection, tumour or head injury. In older adults, a slower form called normal pressure hydrocephalus can develop without an obvious cause. Symptoms In babies: a head that grows unusually fast, a bulging soft spot on the head, vomiting, sleepiness, irritability, or eyes that seem fixed downward. In older children and adults: headache, nausea or vomiting, blurred vision, drowsiness and confusion. Normal pressure hydrocephalus classically causes three things together: difficulty walking, often with a shuffling gait; trouble controlling the bladder; and memory or thinking problems. It is sometimes mistaken for ageing or dementia, which matters because it can often be treated. How it is diagnosed A CT or MRI scan shows enlarged fluid spaces. In babies, an ultrasound through the soft spot can be used. For normal pressure hydrocephalus, doctors may remove some fluid with a needle in the lower back to see whether walking improves. Treatment options A shunt. A t"},{"t":"Head injury","u":"conditions/head-injury.html","k":"Condition","s":"From a mild concussion to bleeding inside the skull. Knowing the warning signs matters.","b":"A head injury can range from a mild bump to serious damage inside the skull. Most are mild and get better on their own, but some need urgent care, sometimes hours or even weeks after the injury.What it is Concussion is a temporary disturbance of brain function after a blow to the head. It can happen without being knocked out. More serious injuries include a skull fracture or bleeding inside the skull . A bleed can build up quickly, or, especially in older people and those taking blood thinners, slowly over days to weeks. A slow bleed is called a chronic subdural haematoma . Signs that need a scan After any head injury, seek emergency care if there is: Any loss of consciousness, or no memory of the injury Repeated vomiting A headache that keeps getting worse Drowsiness, confusion or unusual behaviour A seizure Clear fluid or blood from the nose or ears Weakness, numbness or difficulty speaking The person takes blood-thinning medicines How it is diagnosed A CT scan is the main test, because it quickly shows bleeding and fractures. Doctors also check alertness, the pupils and limb strength, often repeatedly over several hours. Treatment options Observation. Many people are watched in hospital for a period, because some problems only appear after a few hours. Surgery. A large blood clot pressing on the brain may need to be removed through an opening in the skull. A chronic subdural"},{"t":"Trigeminal neuralgia","u":"conditions/trigeminal-neuralgia.html","k":"Condition","s":"Sudden, severe, electric-shock-like pain in one side of the face.","b":"Trigeminal neuralgia causes sudden attacks of severe, sharp, electric-shock-like pain in one side of the face. Many people first see a dentist, because the pain can feel like toothache.What it is The trigeminal nerve carries feeling from the face to the brain. In trigeminal neuralgia this nerve sends sudden bursts of pain signals. The most common cause is a blood vessel pressing on the nerve close to where it joins the brainstem. Less often it is linked to multiple sclerosis or, rarely, a tumour. Symptoms Stabbing or electric-shock pain in the cheek, jaw, teeth, gums, lips or around the eye, usually on one side Attacks lasting from a second to about two minutes, sometimes in rapid clusters Pain triggered by light touch, chewing, talking, brushing teeth, shaving or a breeze Pain-free periods between attacks, which can last weeks or months How it is diagnosed The diagnosis is mainly made from the description of the pain. An MRI scan is usually done to look for a blood vessel touching the nerve and to rule out other causes. Treatment options Medicines first. Certain anti-seizure medicines, such as carbamazepine, calm the nerve and control pain for many people. Ordinary painkillers usually don't help. Microvascular decompression. An operation behind the ear to move the blood vessel away from the nerve and place a small cushion between them. It treats the cause and keeps feeling in "},{"t":"Slipped disc and sciatica","u":"conditions/slipped-disc.html","k":"Condition","s":"A disc pressing on a nerve, often causing leg pain, tingling or numbness.","b":"A 'slipped disc' is a common cause of back and leg pain. Despite the name, nothing actually slips out of place, and most people get better without an operation.What it is Between the bones of the spine are discs : tough outer rings with a soft, jelly-like centre. If the outer ring weakens, the centre can push out. Doctors call this a disc prolapse or herniation . If the bulge presses on a nearby nerve, it causes pain along that nerve's path. When this affects the lower back, the pain runs down the leg and is called sciatica . In the neck, it causes arm pain. Symptoms Leg pain that is often worse than the back pain, sometimes reaching the foot Numbness, tingling or pins and needles in the leg or foot Weakness in the leg or foot Pain that is worse when sitting, bending, coughing or sneezing How it is diagnosed A doctor can often make the diagnosis from your symptoms and an examination. An MRI scan is usually done if symptoms are severe, aren't improving after several weeks, or if surgery is being considered. Disc bulges are very common on scans of people with no pain at all, so the scan is only part of the picture. Treatment options Staying active. Gentle movement and walking usually help more than bed rest. Pain relief and physiotherapy to manage symptoms while the disc settles. Nerve root injections can reduce severe pain for some people. Surgery. A microdiscectomy removes the "},{"t":"Lumbar canal stenosis","u":"conditions/lumbar-canal-stenosis.html","k":"Condition","s":"Narrowing of the spinal canal in the lower back, causing leg pain when walking.","b":"Lumbar canal stenosis means the space for the nerves in the lower back has become narrower, usually because of age-related wear. It typically causes pain or heaviness in the legs when walking or standing.What it is Over the years, discs can bulge, ligaments can thicken and small bony growths can form around the spine's joints. Together, these can squeeze the nerves running down the spinal canal. Stenosis simply means narrowing. Symptoms Pain, heaviness, numbness or weakness in the buttocks and legs that comes on with walking or standing Relief from sitting down or bending forward. Many people notice they can walk further leaning on a shopping trolley A walking distance that gradually gets shorter How it is diagnosed An MRI scan shows the narrowing. Doctors may also check the circulation in your legs, because poor blood flow can cause similar leg pain on walking. Treatment options Non-surgical care. Physiotherapy, exercises such as cycling that keep the spine slightly bent forward, pain relief and sometimes injections. Decompression surgery. An operation, often called a laminectomy , removes the bone and thickened ligament pressing on the nerves to make more space. Fusion. If the spine is unstable, the surgeon may also join two bones together using screws and rods. Surgery is usually considered when symptoms seriously limit daily life despite non-surgical treatment."},{"t":"Cervical myelopathy","u":"conditions/cervical-myelopathy.html","k":"Condition","s":"Pressure on the spinal cord in the neck, causing clumsy hands and unsteady walking.","b":"Cervical myelopathy happens when the spinal cord is squeezed in the neck. It often develops slowly and can be mistaken for normal ageing, so it is easy to miss.What it is The spinal cord runs through a channel in the neck bones. Wear and tear, bulging discs, thickened ligaments or bony growths can narrow this channel and press on the cord. In some people a ligament behind the spine gradually turns to bone, a condition called OPLL . This differs from a pinched nerve root in the neck ( radiculopathy ), which mainly causes arm pain. Symptoms Clumsy hands: difficulty with buttons, writing, or picking up small objects Dropping things Unsteady walking or poor balance Numbness or tingling in the hands Sometimes changes in bladder control Neck pain may be mild or absent. How it is diagnosed An examination often shows brisk reflexes and other signs of spinal cord pressure. An MRI scan confirms where and how much the cord is compressed. Treatment options Myelopathy usually progresses over time. The main aim of surgery is to stop it getting worse. How much lost function returns varies from person to person, which is why timing matters. Surgery from the front of the neck, such as removing the disc and fusing the bones ( ACDF ). Surgery from the back of the neck, such as widening the canal ( laminoplasty ) or a laminectomy with fusion. Careful monitoring may be reasonable in very mild cases"},{"t":"Cauda equina syndrome","u":"conditions/cauda-equina-syndrome.html","k":"Condition","s":"A spinal emergency where nerves controlling the bladder and bowel are squeezed.","b":"Cauda equina syndrome is a spinal emergency. It needs to be assessed in hospital the same day, because delay can cause permanent problems with bladder, bowel and sexual function.What it is At the bottom of the spine, the nerves spread out into a bundle that looks a little like a horse's tail, which is what cauda equina means in Latin. These nerves control the bladder, bowel, sexual function and parts of the legs. If something presses hard on this bundle, most often a large slipped disc, the nerves can be damaged quickly. Warning signs Numbness or altered feeling around the groin, genitals, buttocks or inner thighs (the area that would touch a saddle) New difficulty passing urine, or not being able to feel when the bladder is full Loss of control of the bladder or bowel New problems with sexual function Weakness or numbness in both legs, often with back pain and sciatica Any one of the first three, especially alongside back or leg pain, needs emergency assessment. What to do Go to an emergency department straight away and say that you are worried about cauda equina syndrome. An urgent MRI scan is needed. If it is confirmed, surgery to relieve the pressure on the nerves is usually done as soon as possible. After treatment Recovery depends largely on how much nerve damage happened before the pressure was relieved. Some people recover fully, while others have lasting bladder, bowel"},{"t":"Spinal tuberculosis","u":"conditions/spinal-tuberculosis.html","k":"Condition","s":"A TB infection of the spine. Common in India, and treatable with medicines.","b":"Spinal tuberculosis is a TB infection in the bones of the spine. It is common in India, it develops slowly, and with the right treatment most people recover well.What it is Tuberculosis usually affects the lungs, but it can spread through the blood to other parts of the body, including the spine. This is sometimes called Pott's spine . The infection can weaken the bones and, if untreated, cause the spine to bend forward or press on the nerves. Symptoms Back or neck pain that builds up over weeks or months, often worse at night Fever, night sweats, weight loss and tiredness Later, a visible hump or bend in the back Weakness, numbness or tingling in the legs if the nerves are affected How it is diagnosed An MRI scan shows the infection and any pressure on the nerves. Blood tests help, and a biopsy , taking a small sample from the spine, is often needed to confirm TB and check which medicines will work. Treatment options Anti-TB medicines are the main treatment, usually taken for many months. Completing the full course is essential, even when you feel better, to prevent the infection returning or becoming drug-resistant. A brace may be used to support the spine while it heals. Surgery may be needed if there is pressure on the spinal cord or nerves, a worsening deformity, an unstable spine, or if the diagnosis is uncertain. In India, TB testing and treatment are available free of c"},{"t":"Stroke","u":"conditions/stroke.html","k":"Condition","s":"A blocked or burst blood vessel in the brain. Every minute counts.","b":"A stroke happens when the blood supply to part of the brain is suddenly cut off, either by a clot blocking an artery or by an artery bursting. It is a medical emergency, and the faster treatment starts, the more of the brain can be saved.What it is There are two main types. Ischaemic stroke (about four in five strokes). A clot blocks an artery, and the brain beyond it is starved of oxygen. The centre of the affected area is damaged quickly, but the area around it can often be saved for a few hours if blood flow returns. Haemorrhagic stroke. An artery bursts and bleeds into or around the brain. Bleeding deep inside the brain is often linked to long-standing high blood pressure. Bleeding around the brain is often from a burst aneurysm. A transient ischaemic attack (TIA) , or mini-stroke, causes the same symptoms but they go away within minutes to hours. It is a warning sign that a full stroke may follow, and it needs urgent assessment too. Recognising a stroke: think FAST F ace: has one side of the face drooped? Can they smile evenly? A rms: can they lift both arms and keep them there? S peech: is their speech slurred, muddled, or have they lost it? T ime: call 112 immediately, and note the time the symptoms started. Other sudden symptoms include loss of vision, double vision, severe dizziness with unsteadiness, confusion, or a sudden severe headache. How it is diagnosed An urgen"},{"t":"Pituitary tumour","u":"conditions/pituitary-tumour.html","k":"Condition","s":"A usually benign growth of the hormone gland below the brain. It can affect vision and hormones.","b":"The pituitary is a gland the size of a pea that sits in a small cup of bone beneath the brain and controls many of the body's hormones. Most pituitary tumours (adenomas) are not cancer, and many can be treated very effectively.What it is A pituitary adenoma is a slow-growing, benign growth of the gland. Some make too much of a hormone ( functioning tumours). Others make no hormone but can grow large enough to press on nearby structures ( non-functioning tumours). Just above the gland, the optic nerves from both eyes cross over. This is why pituitary tumours can affect eyesight. Symptoms From pressure: Gradual loss of the outer edges of vision in both eyes, often first noticed when bumping into things Headaches From too much hormone, depending on the type: Prolactin: irregular or absent periods, milk from the breasts, low sex drive or erectile problems Growth hormone ( acromegaly ): growing hands, feet and jaw, coarse facial features, sweating ACTH ( Cushing's disease ): weight gain around the middle, a rounded face, high blood pressure, diabetes, thin skin From too little hormone: tiredness, feeling cold, low sex drive and low mood. How it is diagnosed An MRI scan focused on the pituitary shows the tumour. Blood tests check every pituitary hormone. A visual field test maps any loss of vision, and is repeated to track it. Treatment options Tablets. Prolactin-secreting tumours (p"},{"t":"Neurocysticercosis","u":"conditions/neurocysticercosis.html","k":"Condition","s":"Cysts of the pork tapeworm in the brain. They can cause seizures, and are usually treatable.","b":"Neurocysticercosis is an infection of the brain with the larval stage of the pork tapeworm. It is one of the most common causes of seizures (fits) in India, and in most people it is very treatable.What it is A person becomes infected by swallowing tapeworm eggs , which come from the stool of someone carrying the adult tapeworm. Eggs spread through contaminated hands, water or uncooked food such as poorly washed vegetables. Vegetarians can be infected too. The eggs hatch in the gut, and the larvae travel through the blood and settle in tissues, including the brain, where they form small cysts. Each cyst passes through stages over months to years before it dies and leaves a small calcified spot. Symptoms Seizures are the most common symptom, often starting in one part of the body or causing brief jerking Headaches Rarely, when cysts block the flow of brain fluid, severe headache, vomiting and drowsiness from raised pressure (hydrocephalus) Many people with cysts have no symptoms at all. How it is diagnosed A CT or MRI scan shows the cysts and their stage. A living cyst can show a tiny dot inside, the head of the larva, which is very characteristic. Calcified spots are best seen on CT. Blood tests can support the diagnosis, and an eye examination is done before treatment to check for cysts in the eye. Treatment options Anti-seizure medicines control fits, usually for at least a ye"},{"t":"Chiari malformation","u":"conditions/chiari-malformation.html","k":"Condition","s":"The lowest part of the brain sits too low, blocking fluid flow at the base of the skull.","b":"In a Chiari malformation, the lowest part of the cerebellum, called the tonsils, extends down through the opening at the base of the skull into the top of the spinal canal. Many people have no symptoms, but some need surgery to make more room.What it is The opening at the base of the skull, the foramen magnum , is where the brainstem joins the spinal cord. Normally the cerebellar tonsils sit just above it. In Chiari malformation type 1, they extend below it, usually because the back of the skull is a little small. The tonsils can crowd the opening and interrupt the flow of cerebrospinal fluid. In some people, fluid collects inside the spinal cord, forming a cavity called a syrinx . Symptoms Headache at the back of the head or neck, typically brought on by coughing, sneezing, laughing or straining Dizziness, unsteadiness or poor coordination Difficulty swallowing, or a hoarse voice With a syrinx: numbness, tingling or weakness in the hands and arms, often with loss of temperature feeling, and sometimes a curving spine (scoliosis) Many Chiari malformations are found by chance on scans and never cause symptoms. How it is diagnosed An MRI scan of the brain and spine shows how low the tonsils sit and whether there is a syrinx. Special MRI sequences can show the fluid flow. Treatment options Monitoring. If there are no symptoms and no syrinx, surgery is usually not needed. Posterior "},{"t":"Parkinson's disease and DBS","u":"conditions/parkinsons-dbs.html","k":"Condition","s":"Tremor, stiffness and slowness, and how deep brain stimulation can help.","b":"Parkinson's disease is a slowly progressive condition of the brain that affects movement. It is treated mainly with medicines, but for some people a neurosurgical treatment called deep brain stimulation (DBS) can make a big difference.What it is Deep in the brain, a group of cells called the substantia nigra makes dopamine, a chemical messenger that keeps movement smooth. In Parkinson's disease these cells slowly die. Movement circuits then fire in an abnormal rhythm. Symptoms A tremor , typically at rest, often starting in one hand Stiffness of the muscles Slowness of movement, with small handwriting, a quieter voice and less facial expression A shuffling walk and problems with balance later on Non-movement symptoms such as loss of smell, constipation, acting out dreams, low mood and sleep problems, which can begin years earlier How it is diagnosed The diagnosis is made by a neurologist from the history and examination. Scans are mainly used to rule out other causes. A good response to Parkinson's medicine supports the diagnosis. Treatment options Medicines such as levodopa replace or mimic dopamine and work well for many years for most people. Exercise, physiotherapy and speech therapy help keep movement and voice strong. Deep brain stimulation (DBS). Fine electrodes are placed in a precise target deep in the brain, usually the subthalamic nucleus, and connected to a battery "},{"t":"Carpal tunnel syndrome","u":"conditions/carpal-tunnel-syndrome.html","k":"Condition","s":"A squeezed nerve at the wrist causing tingling and numbness in the fingers, often at night.","b":"Carpal tunnel syndrome is the most common trapped nerve. The median nerve is squeezed as it passes through a narrow tunnel at the wrist, causing tingling, numbness and pain in the hand, typically at night.What it is The carpal tunnel is a narrow channel at the front of the wrist. Its floor and walls are wrist bones, and its roof is a tough band called the transverse carpal ligament. Nine tendons that bend the fingers and thumb pass through it, together with the median nerve . When the contents swell or the space narrows, the nerve is squeezed. It is more common in women, in pregnancy, and in people with diabetes, an underactive thyroid, rheumatoid arthritis or jobs with repetitive wrist movements. Symptoms Tingling, numbness or pins and needles in the thumb, index, middle and half of the ring finger Symptoms that wake you at night and ease when you shake or hang the hand Pain that can spread up the forearm Clumsiness and dropping things Later, weakness and wasting of the muscle at the base of the thumb The little finger is usually not affected, which helps to tell it apart from a trapped nerve in the neck. How it is diagnosed The diagnosis is mostly made from the symptoms and an examination. Nerve conduction studies measure how well signals pass through the wrist and show how severe the compression is. An ultrasound scan is sometimes used. Treatment options A wrist splint at ni"},{"t":"Cervical radiculopathy","u":"conditions/cervical-radiculopathy.html","k":"Condition","s":"A pinched nerve in the neck, causing pain, numbness or weakness down one arm.","b":"Cervical radiculopathy means a nerve root in the neck is being squeezed, usually by a disc bulge or a bony spur. It causes pain, tingling, numbness or weakness travelling down one arm. Each root supplies its own strip of the arm, so the pattern of symptoms shows which root is affected.What it is Eight pairs of nerve roots leave the spinal cord in the neck, each passing through a small opening between two vertebrae. A disc bulge (common in younger adults) or bony spurs from wear and tear (common after 50) can narrow this opening and press on the root. This is different from cervical myelopathy , where the spinal cord itself is squeezed. Symptoms Sharp, burning or aching pain spreading from the neck into the shoulder blade, arm and hand, often worse than the neck pain Tingling or numbness in a particular strip of the arm and fingers Weakness of particular movements, such as lifting the arm, bending or straightening the elbow, or gripping Pain that eases when you rest your hand on top of your head How it is diagnosed An examination of feeling, strength and reflexes in the arm usually points to the root involved. An MRI scan shows the disc or bone pressing on it. Nerve conduction studies can help when the cause is unclear. Treatment options Non-surgical care. Pain relief, nerve pain medicines, physiotherapy and staying active. Nerve root injection of steroid around the root can eas"},{"t":"Spondylolisthesis","u":"conditions/spondylolisthesis.html","k":"Condition","s":"One vertebra slipping forward on the one below, which can squeeze the nerves.","b":"Spondylolisthesis means one vertebra has slipped forward over the one below it. Many people with a slip have little or no trouble, but it can cause back pain and squeeze the nerves to the legs.What it is There are two common types. Degenerative. In older adults, usually at the L4–L5 level, worn facet joints and a weakened disc allow the vertebra to slide forward. Isthmic. In younger people, usually at L5, a stress fracture in a thin part of the back of the vertebra (the pars , often from sports that bend the back repeatedly) lets the vertebra slip. Slips are graded by how far the vertebra has moved, from grade I (less than a quarter) upward. Symptoms Lower back pain, often worse with standing and activity Leg pain, heaviness, numbness or tingling on walking, eased by sitting or bending forward Pain shooting down one leg if a nerve root is pinched Tight hamstring muscles in younger people How it is diagnosed X-rays , including views taken while bending forward and backward, show the slip and whether it moves. An MRI scan shows pressure on the nerves. A CT scan shows a pars fracture clearly. Treatment options Non-surgical care helps most people: physiotherapy focused on core strength, pain relief, activity modification and sometimes injections. Decompression and fusion. If symptoms seriously limit daily life despite this, an operation frees the squeezed nerves and joins the verte"},{"t":"Osteoporotic spine fracture","u":"conditions/osteoporotic-spine-fracture.html","k":"Condition","s":"A vertebra weakened by thin bones collapses, often after a minor fall or even a cough.","b":"With osteoporosis, bones become thin and fragile. A vertebra can crack and collapse after a minor fall, lifting something, or sometimes with no clear injury at all. It is a common cause of sudden back pain in older people, and a warning sign that the bones need treatment.What it is Inside each vertebra is a honeycomb of bone that carries the weight of the body. In osteoporosis this honeycomb thins. The front of the vertebra, which carries the most load, can collapse into a wedge shape. This is called a vertebral compression fracture . It is most common in women after the menopause, older men, and people who have taken steroid tablets for a long time. Symptoms Sudden back pain, usually in the middle or lower back, worse when standing or walking and eased by lying down Loss of height A gradually stooped or rounded back after several fractures Some fractures cause little pain and are found by chance on an X-ray. How it is diagnosed An X-ray shows the collapsed vertebra. An MRI scan shows whether a fracture is new, and helps rule out other causes such as infection or cancer. A bone density (DEXA) scan confirms osteoporosis. Treatment options Most fractures heal over 6 to 12 weeks with pain relief, a short period of reduced activity, sometimes a brace, and then a gradual return to walking. Vertebroplasty or kyphoplasty. If pain stays severe, bone cement can be injected into the frac"},{"t":"Craniosynostosis","u":"conditions/craniosynostosis.html","k":"Condition","s":"When the joints of a baby's skull fuse too early, giving the head an unusual shape.","b":"A baby's skull is made of several bony plates joined by flexible seams called sutures. In craniosynostosis, one or more sutures fuse too early. The skull cannot grow normally at that seam, so it grows in other directions and takes on a characteristic shape.What it is The sutures stay open in infancy so the skull can expand as the brain grows rapidly. When a suture fuses early, growth across it stops and the skull compensates by growing more elsewhere. The type is named after the suture involved: sagittal (the most common), metopic , coronal or, rarely, lambdoid . Most cases happen on their own. Some, especially when several sutures fuse, are part of a genetic syndrome. A much more common cause of a flat head is positional plagiocephaly , flattening from lying in the same position. It is not craniosynostosis, does not need surgery, and usually improves with repositioning and tummy time. Signs An unusual head shape noticed at birth or in the first months, such as a long narrow head, a pointed forehead, or a forehead flattened on one side A hard ridge along a suture A soft spot (fontanelle) that closes early Rarely, signs of raised pressure: irritability, vomiting or poor feeding How it is diagnosed An experienced doctor can usually tell from the head shape. A low-dose CT scan with 3D images confirms which sutures are fused and helps plan surgery. Treatment options Endoscopic stri"},{"t":"Spina bifida","u":"conditions/spina-bifida.html","k":"Condition","s":"The back of the spine does not close fully before birth. Severity ranges from harmless to serious.","b":"Spina bifida means the bony arch at the back of one or more vertebrae did not close properly during the first month of pregnancy. It ranges from a hidden gap that causes no problems to an open defect involving the spinal cord, which needs surgery soon after birth.What it is Spina bifida occulta. A small gap in the bony arch, with normal skin and nerves. It is common and usually harmless. Meningocele. A skin-covered sac of fluid pushes out through the gap, but the spinal cord stays in place. Myelomeningocele. The most serious form. The spinal cord and nerves are exposed in an open sac on the back. A related group of conditions, closed or occult spinal dysraphism , includes a tethered spinal cord, where the bottom of the cord is stuck down and stretched as the child grows. Clues on the skin include a dimple, tuft of hair, birthmark or fatty lump over the lower spine. Effects In myelomeningocele, effects depend on the level of the defect and may include: Weakness or paralysis of the legs, and reduced feeling Problems with bladder and bowel control Hydrocephalus , a build-up of fluid in the brain, in many babies, often needing a shunt A Chiari type 2 malformation at the base of the brain Foot and hip problems and a curved spine How it is diagnosed Myelomeningocele is usually seen on the anomaly ultrasound scan during pregnancy. After birth, an MRI scan shows the spinal cord in deta"},{"t":"Headache","u":"conditions/headache.html","k":"Condition","s":"Primary, secondary and referred headaches: what each feels like, the warning signs, and when to see a specialist.","b":"Almost everyone has headaches. Most are not dangerous and are caused by the headache itself, such as tension-type headache or migraine. A few are a sign of another problem that needs treatment. Knowing the type, and the warning signs, tells you what to do next.What a headache is The brain itself can't feel pain. Headache comes from the structures around it: the blood vessels, the coverings of the brain (the meninges), and the nerves, muscles and joints of the head and neck. Doctors sort headaches into groups, because the group decides the treatment. Primary headaches. The headache is the condition itself, with no other disease behind it. Tension-type headache, migraine and cluster headache are the main ones. They are by far the most common. Secondary headaches. The headache is a symptom of something else. Usually that is minor, such as a cold, fever, a hangover or too much caffeine. Less often it is a problem that needs urgent treatment, such as bleeding, infection or raised pressure inside the head. Referred headaches. A type of secondary headache in which the pain starts in a nearby part, such as the neck, jaw, teeth, sinuses or eyes, and is felt in the head. Choose a type below to see where it usually hurts and how it behaves over time. Real headaches don't always follow the textbook, so use this as a guide, not a diagnosis. Primary headaches Tension-type headache. A pressin"},{"t":"Brachial plexus injury","u":"conditions/brachial-plexus-injury.html","k":"Condition","s":"Injury to the network of nerves between the neck and the arm, often after a motorcycle crash or during birth.","b":"The brachial plexus is the network of nerves that runs from the neck, behind the collarbone and into the armpit, and controls the whole arm. When it is stretched, torn or pulled from the spinal cord, part or all of the arm can become weak, numb and painful. Some injuries recover on their own. Others need surgery, and timing matters.What it is Five nerve roots, called C5, C6, C7, C8 and T1, leave the spinal cord between the bones of the lower neck and upper chest. They join, split and regroup in a set pattern, and end as the main nerves of the arm. Together they move the shoulder, elbow, wrist and hand, and carry feeling from the skin. Because each root supplies particular muscles, the pattern of weakness shows which roots are injured. Choose a pattern below to see which nerves are affected and which movements stop working. Causes Road accidents , especially motorcycle crashes, are the most common cause in adults. The head and shoulder are forced apart, stretching or tearing the nerves. Falls onto the shoulder, and sports injuries. Cuts and gunshot wounds near the neck or armpit. Birth. The nerves can be stretched during a difficult delivery, for example when a baby's shoulder gets stuck. This affects about 1 to 2 in every 1,000 births. Less often , a tumour pressing on the nerves, radiotherapy to the area, or inflammation of the nerves. Types of injury How badly the nerve is da"},{"t":"Warning signs that need emergency care","u":"resources/emergency-warning-signs.html","k":"Guide","s":"The brain and spine symptoms that should never wait for an appointment.","b":"Most brain and spine symptoms can be assessed in a normal clinic. A few cannot. If you notice any of the signs below, treat them as an emergency.Brain warning signs A sudden, severe headache that reaches full strength within seconds or a minute Sudden weakness or numbness of the face, arm or leg, especially on one side Sudden difficulty speaking or understanding speech Sudden loss of vision or double vision A seizure for the first time Confusion, drowsiness or repeated vomiting after a head injury Spine warning signs Numbness around the groin, genitals, buttocks or inner thighs New difficulty passing urine, or loss of bladder or bowel control Weakness in both legs, or weakness that is quickly getting worse Back pain with fever, unexplained weight loss, or a history of cancer Arm or leg weakness after a fall or injury to the neck or back What to do Call 112 , or go to the nearest hospital emergency department. Tell them which of these symptoms you have and when they started. If possible, bring a list of your medicines and any previous scan reports. Please don't use email or social media messages for these symptoms. They may not be seen in time."},{"t":"Preparing for neurosurgery","u":"resources/preparing-for-surgery.html","k":"Guide","s":"What to gather, what to ask, and how to plan before an operation.","b":"Preparing well before brain or spine surgery can make the day calmer and recovery smoother. Your surgical team will give you instructions specific to you. This page covers the general steps.What to gather All scan reports, and the actual images on CD, film or a link Previous discharge summaries and test results A written list of every medicine you take, including doses, supplements and herbal remedies Details of any allergies Tell the team especially about blood thinners and diabetes medicines. Some need to be stopped or adjusted before surgery, but only on medical advice. Understanding the operation Before you sign a consent form, you should understand what the operation involves, why it is recommended, the main risks, and what alternatives exist, including not operating. It is completely reasonable to ask for things to be explained again. Planning ahead Follow the fasting instructions you are given exactly Arrange for someone to stay with you or help at home for the first days Plan time off work, and ask how long recovery usually takes If you smoke, stopping, even for a short time before surgery, helps healing On the day Bring your documents, medicine list and comfortable, loose clothing. Leave valuables at home. Expect to see several members of the team who will check your details, which is a normal safety step."},{"t":"Understanding your MRI report","u":"resources/reading-your-mri-report.html","k":"Guide","s":"Plain-language meanings for the words that often worry people most.","b":"MRI reports are written for doctors, so they can sound alarming. Many common findings are normal for your age. This guide explains frequent terms, but your doctor should always interpret your report in the light of your symptoms.Common spine terms Degenerative changes Age-related wear in the spine. Very common, even in people with no pain. Disc bulge A disc extending slightly beyond its normal edge. Often found in people without symptoms. Disc protrusion or herniation The soft centre of a disc pushing out further. It matters most if it presses on a nerve that matches your symptoms. Stenosis Narrowing of a space, such as the spinal canal or the openings where nerves exit. Nerve root compression A nerve being pressed on. Whether it matters depends on whether it fits your symptoms. Cord compression Pressure on the spinal cord itself. This needs a specialist opinion. Common brain terms Lesion A general word for any area that looks different from normal. It does not mean cancer. Hyperintensity An area that appears brighter on certain scan sequences. Small ones are common with age. Enhancement An area that lights up after contrast dye is given. It helps doctors narrow down what something might be. Incidental finding Something found by chance, unrelated to why the scan was done. Most are harmless. Clinical correlation suggested The radiologist is asking your doctor to compare the find"},{"t":"Questions to ask your neurosurgeon","u":"resources/questions-to-ask.html","k":"Guide","s":"A checklist to take into your appointment, so nothing important is missed.","b":"Appointments can feel rushed, and it's easy to forget questions. Writing them down beforehand helps. Bring someone with you if you can, and take notes.About the diagnosis What exactly is the problem, in simple words? What is causing my symptoms? Do I need any more tests? About the options What are all my treatment options, including not having surgery? What happens if I wait? Why are you recommending this option for me? If surgery is recommended What does the operation involve? What are the main risks, and how common are they? What improvement can I realistically expect? How long will I be in hospital, and how long is recovery? When can I return to work, drive and exercise? After treatment What warning signs should I watch for at home? Who do I contact if I'm worried? When is my follow-up?"},{"t":"Recovering after spine surgery","u":"resources/recovering-after-spine-surgery.html","k":"Guide","s":"General guidance on walking, wound care and warning signs in the first weeks.","b":"Recovery after spine surgery is different for everyone and depends on the operation you had. Always follow your own team's advice. These are general principles that apply to many people.Moving and walking Most people are encouraged to get up and walk soon after surgery, often the same or next day. Short, regular walks help recovery. Increase the distance gradually. Caring for your wound Keep the wound clean and dry as instructed Don't apply creams unless advised Ask when stitches or clips need removing Lifting, driving and work Your team will advise when you can lift, bend, drive and return to work. This varies widely depending on the operation and your job, so ask for specific guidance rather than relying on general rules. Signs to report Increasing redness, swelling, discharge or bleeding from the wound Fever New or worsening weakness or numbness A headache that is worse when sitting or standing and eases lying down Pain in the calf, or breathlessness Problems with bladder or bowel control, or numbness around the groin, need emergency care."},{"t":"Getting a second opinion","u":"resources/second-opinion.html","k":"Guide","s":"Why a second opinion is normal, and how to get the most from one.","b":"Asking for a second opinion is a normal and sensible part of making a big decision about your health. Good doctors expect it and won't be offended.When it can help Surgery has been recommended and you're unsure You've been given different advice by different people Your diagnosis is rare or complex You want more confidence before going ahead How to prepare Collect your scan images, not just the reports Bring discharge summaries, test results and your medicine list Write a short timeline of your symptoms Prepare the specific questions you want answered If the opinions differ Ask each doctor to explain their reasoning. Sometimes there is more than one reasonable option, and the right choice depends on what matters most to you."},{"t":"Seizure first aid","u":"resources/seizure-first-aid.html","k":"Guide","s":"What to do, and what not to do, when someone has a fit.","b":"Watching someone have a seizure (fit) is frightening, but most stop on their own within a couple of minutes. Simple first aid keeps the person safe until it passes.What to do Stay calm and stay with them. Note the time the seizure started. Protect them from injury. Move hard or sharp objects away. Cushion their head with something soft, such as a folded jacket. Loosen anything tight around the neck, and remove glasses. Once the jerking stops, roll them onto their side (the recovery position) so saliva can drain and the airway stays open. Stay until they are fully awake. Confusion and sleepiness afterwards are normal. Speak gently and reassure them. What not to do Don't put anything in their mouth. They cannot swallow their tongue, and objects can break teeth or block the airway. Don't hold them down or try to stop the movements. Don't give food or water until they are fully alert. Don't use smelling salts, onions or shoes. They don't help. Call 112 if The seizure lasts more than five minutes One seizure follows another without the person waking up in between It is the person's first seizure They are injured, pregnant, have diabetes, or had the seizure in water They have trouble breathing, or don't wake up afterwards Afterwards Write down what you saw: how it started, which parts of the body moved, how long it lasted and how the person was afterwards. A phone video, taken only i"},{"t":"After a head injury: care at home","u":"resources/head-injury-care-at-home.html","k":"Guide","s":"How to watch someone at home after a bump to the head, and when to return to hospital.","b":"Most people with a mild head injury are allowed home, often after a scan. The first two days matter most. This guide explains what to watch for and how to return to normal activity safely.The first 48 hours Have a responsible adult stay with the person for at least the first 24 hours. Rest, but there is no need to keep them awake. It is fine to sleep; check that they are easy to wake and behaving normally. Paracetamol can be used for headache. Avoid alcohol and sleeping tablets. Avoid driving, contact sport and heavy work until a doctor says it is safe. Return to hospital or call 112 if Increasing drowsiness, or difficulty waking up Headache that keeps getting worse Vomiting more than once Confusion, unusual behaviour, slurred speech or difficulty understanding A seizure Weakness or numbness in an arm or leg, or problems with vision or balance Clear fluid or blood coming from the nose or ears Delayed problems In older people and those taking blood thinners, a slow bleed (chronic subdural haematoma) can appear days to weeks later. New confusion, headaches, unsteadiness or weakness after a fall, even a minor one, should be checked. Getting back to normal After a short period of rest, increase activity gradually: light walking and screen time first, then work or study, then exercise. If symptoms worsen, step back for a day. Return to contact sport only when fully free of symptoms "},{"t":"Having a CT or MRI scan","u":"resources/having-a-scan.html","k":"Guide","s":"What each scan shows, how to prepare, and what it feels like.","b":"CT and MRI are the two main scans used for the brain and spine. Knowing what to expect makes the scan easier, and a little preparation avoids delays.CT or MRI? CT scan Uses X-rays. It takes only a few minutes and is the first test in emergencies, because it quickly shows bleeding, fractures and calcified spots. It involves a small dose of radiation. MRI scan Uses a strong magnet and radio waves, with no radiation. It shows the brain, spinal cord, nerves and discs in much greater detail, but takes 20 to 45 minutes and is noisy. Before the scan For MRI, tell the staff about any metal in your body: a pacemaker, cochlear implant, aneurysm clip, shunt valve, metal fragments in the eyes, or recent surgery with implants. Many implants are safe, but the team must check first. Remove jewellery, hairpins, watches, credit cards and phones. If dye (contrast) may be used, tell them about kidney problems, diabetes medicines, allergies, previous reactions to dye, and pregnancy. Bring previous scans so the new images can be compared. During the scan You lie still on a table that moves into the scanner. Keeping still gives the clearest pictures. MRI makes loud knocking noises, so you'll be given ear protection, and you can talk to the staff through an intercom. If you feel claustrophobic, say so when booking. Bringing someone with you, closing your eyes, music, or sometimes a mild sedative can "},{"t":"Blood thinners and brain or spine surgery","u":"resources/blood-thinners-and-surgery.html","k":"Guide","s":"Why these medicines matter, and why you should never stop them on your own.","b":"Many people take medicines that thin the blood, to prevent strokes or heart attacks. Before brain or spine surgery they often need to be paused, but stopping them without advice can be dangerous.Which medicines Antiplatelets: aspirin, clopidogrel, prasugrel, ticagrelor Anticoagulants: warfarin, acenocoumarol, apixaban, rivaroxaban, dabigatran, and heparin injections Some painkillers (such as ibuprofen) and supplements (such as fish oil or high-dose vitamin E) can also affect bleeding Why they matter Bleeding inside the skull or spinal canal, even a small amount, can press on the brain or nerves. So surgeons usually want blood clotting to be as normal as possible during and after the operation. At the same time, these medicines protect against clots. For someone with a recent heart stent, an artificial heart valve or a recent clot, stopping them can be risky. Making a plan Never stop a blood thinner on your own. The decision is made together by your surgeon and the doctor who prescribed it. Bring the exact names and doses to your appointment, or the strips themselves. You'll be told when to stop, whether you need a temporary injection instead, and when to restart after surgery. In an emergency After any head injury or fall, tell the hospital straight away if you take a blood thinner. A scan is usually advised even if you feel well, and some medicines can be reversed quickly."},{"t":"Recovering after brain surgery","u":"resources/recovering-after-brain-surgery.html","k":"Guide","s":"Wound care, headaches, tiredness, driving and the warning signs to watch for.","b":"Recovery after brain surgery varies a great deal depending on the operation. Your team will give you specific advice. This guide covers what is common for most people in the first weeks.Your wound Keep it clean and dry until you are told you can wash your hair, usually after a few days. Pat dry gently. Don't apply oils, creams or hair dye until it has fully healed. Stitches or clips are usually removed at 7 to 14 days. Some swelling around the eyes and face, and numbness around the scar, are common and settle with time. How you may feel Tiredness is the most common complaint and can last weeks to months. Plan rest periods during the day. Headaches usually ease week by week with simple painkillers. Concentration, memory and mood can take time to return to normal. This is common and usually improves. Medicines Take steroids and anti-seizure medicines exactly as prescribed. Steroids are usually reduced gradually and must not be stopped suddenly. Ask before restarting any blood thinners. Getting back to normal Walk a little more each day. Avoid heavy lifting, swimming and contact sport until your surgeon allows them. Driving: don't drive until your doctor says it is safe. After seizures or some types of brain surgery, there are legal rules about driving. Return to work gradually, often part-time at first. Seek urgent help if Increasing drowsiness, confusion, or a severe or worsenin"},{"t":"Living with a shunt","u":"resources/living-with-a-shunt.html","k":"Guide","s":"Everyday life with a shunt, and the warning signs that it may be blocked or infected.","b":"A shunt drains extra fluid from the brain, usually into the abdomen. Most people with a shunt live full, active lives. The key is knowing the warning signs of a problem, because shunts can block or become infected, sometimes years later.Know your shunt Keep a record of the type of shunt and valve, the date it was placed, and the hospital and surgeon. Some valves are programmable and can be changed by strong magnets. Tell the team before any MRI scan; the setting may need to be checked afterwards. Keep copies of recent scans to compare with if a problem is suspected. Signs of a blocked shunt: seek help urgently Headache, especially on waking, with vomiting Drowsiness, irritability or confusion Blurred or double vision, or eyes that look downward In babies: a bulging soft spot, a fast-growing head, poor feeding or unusual sleepiness A return of the symptoms the person had before the shunt was placed Signs of infection Fever, redness or swelling along the shunt tubing, tummy pain, or a wound that leaks. Infection is most common in the first few months after surgery. Everyday life Most sports and activities are fine, though head-on contact sports may need discussion. Travel, including flying, is fine. Carry a card or a note on your phone that says you have a shunt, in case you need care away from home."},{"t":"Looking after your back","u":"resources/looking-after-your-back.html","k":"Guide","s":"What genuinely helps back and neck pain, and when to see a doctor.","b":"Back and neck pain are extremely common, and most episodes settle within a few weeks. The spine is strong. Staying active, rather than resting, is usually the most helpful thing you can do.What helps Keep moving. Gentle walking and normal daily activity speed recovery. Bed rest for more than a day or two usually makes things worse. Short-term pain relief so you can stay active. Ask a pharmacist or doctor what suits you. Heat can ease muscle spasm. Regular exercise such as walking, swimming, yoga or a physiotherapy programme reduces future episodes. Change position often at work. No single sitting posture is perfect; moving is what matters. Common worries \"My disc has slipped out\" Discs don't slip out of place. Bulges are common, often painless, and frequently shrink over time. \"I should avoid bending\" Bending and lifting are normal and safe for most people. Build up gradually after an episode of pain. \"My scan shows degeneration\" Wear-and-tear changes are as normal with age as grey hair, and appear on scans of many people without pain. See a doctor if The pain isn't improving after four to six weeks, it travels down the arm or leg with numbness or weakness, it is worse at night or at rest, or you also have fever, weight loss, or a history of cancer, TB or osteoporosis. Emergency signs Numbness around the groin, buttocks or inner thighs New difficulty passing urine, or loss of b"},{"t":"Caring for a family member","u":"resources/caring-for-a-family-member.html","k":"Guide","s":"Practical support for families of someone with a brain or spine condition.","b":"When someone you love has a stroke, a brain tumour, a head injury or major spine surgery, the family often becomes part of the care team. Looking after yourself is part of looking after them.In hospital Choose one family member to be the main contact with the team, and keep a notebook of updates, questions and names. Ask when the doctors usually do their rounds, and what the plan is for the next day or two. Bring a list of the patient's medicines, their usual level of activity, and anything that comforts them. Confusion and agitation are common after brain illness or surgery and often settle. Familiar faces, glasses, hearing aids and a clock help. Going home Before discharge, ask what help will be needed at home, which medicines to give and when, and which warning signs mean returning to hospital. Make the home safe: clear walkways, good lighting, a chair in the bathroom, and grab rails if needed. Ask about physiotherapy, speech therapy and occupational therapy, and how to continue exercises at home. Changes in behaviour and mood After a brain injury or stroke, people can become more tired, irritable, forgetful or low in mood. These changes come from the brain injury, not from a lack of effort. They often improve with time, and doctors can help with some of them. Looking after yourself Caring is tiring. Share tasks among family members, accept offers of help, keep up your own h"},{"t":"Head injury in a child","u":"cards/head-injury-child.html","k":"Card","s":"Which signs need an ambulance, which need hospital today, and how to care at home.","b":"Call 112 or 108 now Unconscious, or very hard to wake A fit (seizure) Weakness in an arm or leg Clear fluid or blood from the nose or ears A dent in the skull or a deep wound Go to hospital today Knocked out, even for a few seconds Vomited after the injury Headache that keeps getting worse Confused, very sleepy, or not acting like themselves Can't remember what happened Trouble walking, seeing or talking A baby under 1 with a large swelling or bruise on the head Caring at home Put a cold pack wrapped in a cloth on the bump Give paracetamol if needed Let them sleep, but check they wake easily and know you Watch closely for the next 48 hours No rough play or contact sport until a doctor says it is safe"},{"t":"Cauda equina warning signs","u":"cards/cauda-equina.html","k":"Card","s":"The signs that turn back pain or sciatica into a same-day emergency.","b":"Go to an emergency department now if back pain or sciatica comes with Numbness around the groin, genitals, back passage or inner thighs New difficulty passing urine, or not feeling when you pass urine Loss of bladder or bowel control New problems with erections or sexual sensation Weakness or numbness in both legs, or weakness that is getting worse What to say “I have back pain and these symptoms. I am worried about cauda equina syndrome.” Ask whether you need an urgent MRI scan. Don't wait for a clinic appointment. Early surgery gives the best chance of keeping bladder, bowel and leg function."},{"t":"Stroke: BE FAST","u":"cards/stroke-be-fast.html","k":"Card","s":"Six letters that help you recognise a stroke and get help quickly.","b":"BE FAST: call 112 or 108 now B alance: sudden loss of balance or dizziness E yes: sudden loss of vision or double vision F ace: one side of the face droops A rm: weakness, or one arm drifts down S peech: slurred, muddled, or can't speak or understand T ime: call now, and note the time the symptoms started While you wait Don't give food, drink or tablets If they are drowsy, lay them on their side Even if the symptoms go away, they still need hospital today"},{"t":"Seizure first aid","u":"cards/seizure-first-aid.html","k":"Card","s":"What to do, what not to do, and when to call for help when someone has a fit.","b":"Do Stay calm and note the time it starts Move hard or sharp objects away Cushion their head Loosen anything tight around the neck When the jerking stops, roll them onto their side Stay with them until they are fully awake Don't Put anything in their mouth Hold them down Give food or water until they are fully alert Use onions, shoes or keys Call 112 or 108 if It lasts more than 5 minutes Another seizure starts before they wake up It is their first ever seizure They are injured, pregnant or have diabetes They have trouble breathing afterwards It happened in water"},{"t":"Shunt problems","u":"cards/shunt-problems.html","k":"Card","s":"The warning signs of a blocked or infected shunt, with space for your shunt details.","b":"Go to the emergency department now if someone with a shunt has Headache with vomiting Drowsiness, confusion, or is hard to wake A fit (seizure) In a baby: a bulging soft spot, a fast-growing head, vomiting, unusual sleepiness, or eyes fixed downward Fever, or redness or swelling along the tube Tummy pain with fever My shunt details Name Type of shunt and valve Adjustable valve setting Hospital that placed it Neurosurgery team phone"},{"t":"Acute","u":"glossary.html#acute","k":"Glossary","s":"Started recently or suddenly. It describes timing, not how serious something is.","b":""},{"t":"Aneurysm","u":"glossary.html#aneurysm","k":"Glossary","s":"A bulge in a weak spot of a blood vessel wall. In the brain, most never burst.","b":""},{"t":"Angiogram","u":"glossary.html#angiogram","k":"Glossary","s":"A scan or X-ray test that shows blood vessels, usually after a dye is injected.","b":""},{"t":"Annulus","u":"glossary.html#annulus","k":"Glossary","s":"The tough outer ring of a spinal disc. A tear in it can let the soft centre push out.","b":""},{"t":"Arachnoid","u":"glossary.html#arachnoid","k":"Glossary","s":"The thin middle layer of the coverings around the brain and spinal cord.","b":""},{"t":"Atrophy","u":"glossary.html#atrophy","k":"Glossary","s":"Shrinking of a body part. Mild brain atrophy is common with age.","b":""},{"t":"Awake craniotomy","u":"glossary.html#awake-craniotomy","k":"Glossary","s":"Brain surgery where the patient is awake for part of it, so speech or movement can be checked. The scalp is numbed, and the brain itself does not feel pain.","b":""},{"t":"Benign","u":"glossary.html#benign","k":"Glossary","s":"Not cancer. A benign tumour does not spread to other parts of the body, though it can still press on nearby structures.","b":""},{"t":"Biopsy","u":"glossary.html#biopsy","k":"Glossary","s":"Taking a small sample of tissue so it can be examined under a microscope.","b":""},{"t":"Brainstem","u":"glossary.html#brainstem","k":"Glossary","s":"The stalk at the base of the brain that joins it to the spinal cord. It controls breathing, heart rate and consciousness.","b":""},{"t":"Burr hole","u":"glossary.html#burr-hole","k":"Glossary","s":"A small round hole drilled through the skull.","b":""},{"t":"Cauda equina","u":"glossary.html#cauda-equina","k":"Glossary","s":"The bundle of nerve roots at the bottom of the spinal canal, below the end of the spinal cord. The name means 'horse's tail'.","b":""},{"t":"Cerebellum","u":"glossary.html#cerebellum","k":"Glossary","s":"The part of the brain at the back, below the main brain. It controls balance and coordination.","b":""},{"t":"Cerebrospinal fluid (CSF)","u":"glossary.html#cerebrospinal-fluid-csf","k":"Glossary","s":"The clear fluid that surrounds and cushions the brain and spinal cord.","b":""},{"t":"Cervical","u":"glossary.html#cervical","k":"Glossary","s":"To do with the neck. The cervical spine has seven vertebrae, C1 to C7.","b":""},{"t":"Chronic","u":"glossary.html#chronic","k":"Glossary","s":"Long-lasting, usually for more than three months. It describes timing, not how serious something is.","b":""},{"t":"Clinical correlation","u":"glossary.html#clinical-correlation","k":"Glossary","s":"A phrase in scan reports asking the doctor to compare a finding with your symptoms and examination.","b":""},{"t":"Coiling","u":"glossary.html#coiling","k":"Glossary","s":"Treating an aneurysm from inside the blood vessel by filling it with soft platinum coils.","b":""},{"t":"Compression","u":"glossary.html#compression","k":"Glossary","s":"Pressure on a nerve or the spinal cord. Whether it matters depends on whether it fits your symptoms.","b":""},{"t":"Concussion","u":"glossary.html#concussion","k":"Glossary","s":"A mild brain injury that causes temporary symptoms such as headache, dizziness or confusion after a blow to the head.","b":""},{"t":"Contrast","u":"glossary.html#contrast","k":"Glossary","s":"A dye injected during a scan to make blood vessels and some abnormal areas show up more clearly.","b":""},{"t":"Craniotomy","u":"glossary.html#craniotomy","k":"Glossary","s":"An operation in which a piece of the skull is lifted to reach the brain, then put back.","b":""},{"t":"CT scan","u":"glossary.html#ct-scan","k":"Glossary","s":"Computed tomography. A quick X-ray scan that shows bone and fresh bleeding well.","b":""},{"t":"Decompression","u":"glossary.html#decompression","k":"Glossary","s":"Surgery that removes bone, ligament or disc to take pressure off a nerve or the spinal cord.","b":""},{"t":"Degenerative changes","u":"glossary.html#degenerative-changes","k":"Glossary","s":"Wear related to age, seen on many spine scans, including in people with no pain.","b":""},{"t":"Dermatome","u":"glossary.html#dermatome","k":"Glossary","s":"The area of skin supplied by a single spinal nerve root.","b":""},{"t":"Disc","u":"glossary.html#disc","k":"Glossary","s":"The soft cushion between two vertebrae. It has a tough outer ring and a softer centre.","b":""},{"t":"Disc bulge","u":"glossary.html#disc-bulge","k":"Glossary","s":"A disc that spreads a little beyond its normal edge. Very common, and often harmless.","b":""},{"t":"Dura","u":"glossary.html#dura","k":"Glossary","s":"The tough outer covering of the brain and spinal cord.","b":""},{"t":"Endoscope","u":"glossary.html#endoscope","k":"Glossary","s":"A thin tube with a camera and light, used for keyhole surgery.","b":""},{"t":"Enhancement","u":"glossary.html#enhancement","k":"Glossary","s":"An area that lights up on a scan after contrast is given. It helps narrow down what something might be.","b":""},{"t":"Epidural","u":"glossary.html#epidural","k":"Glossary","s":"The space just outside the dura, the covering of the spinal cord.","b":""},{"t":"Extradural haematoma","u":"glossary.html#extradural-haematoma","k":"Glossary","s":"Blood collected between the skull and the dura, usually after a head injury.","b":""},{"t":"Facet joint","u":"glossary.html#facet-joint","k":"Glossary","s":"One of the small paired joints at the back of the spine that link the vertebrae.","b":""},{"t":"Foramen","u":"glossary.html#foramen","k":"Glossary","s":"An opening in bone. Spinal nerve roots leave the spine through openings called foramina.","b":""},{"t":"Frontal lobe","u":"glossary.html#frontal-lobe","k":"Glossary","s":"The front part of the brain. It helps with planning, behaviour, personality and movement.","b":""},{"t":"Fusion","u":"glossary.html#fusion","k":"Glossary","s":"Joining two or more vertebrae so they heal into one solid bone.","b":""},{"t":"GCS (Glasgow Coma Scale)","u":"glossary.html#gcs-glasgow-coma-scale","k":"Glossary","s":"A score from 3 to 15 that measures how awake and responsive a person is, based on eyes, speech and movement.","b":""},{"t":"Glioma","u":"glossary.html#glioma","k":"Glossary","s":"A tumour that grows from the brain's own support cells. Gliomas range from slow-growing to fast-growing.","b":""},{"t":"Haematoma","u":"glossary.html#haematoma","k":"Glossary","s":"A collection of blood outside a blood vessel.","b":""},{"t":"Haemorrhage","u":"glossary.html#haemorrhage","k":"Glossary","s":"Bleeding.","b":""},{"t":"Herniation","u":"glossary.html#herniation","k":"Glossary","s":"When part of a structure pushes out of its normal place, such as the centre of a disc.","b":""},{"t":"Hydrocephalus","u":"glossary.html#hydrocephalus","k":"Glossary","s":"A build-up of fluid inside the brain that raises pressure.","b":""},{"t":"Hyperintensity","u":"glossary.html#hyperintensity","k":"Glossary","s":"An area that looks brighter than its surroundings on certain MRI images. Small ones are common with age.","b":""},{"t":"Incidental finding","u":"glossary.html#incidental-finding","k":"Glossary","s":"Something found by chance on a scan done for another reason. Most are harmless.","b":""},{"t":"Infarct","u":"glossary.html#infarct","k":"Glossary","s":"An area of tissue damaged because its blood supply was cut off, as in a stroke.","b":""},{"t":"Intracranial pressure","u":"glossary.html#intracranial-pressure","k":"Glossary","s":"The pressure inside the skull.","b":""},{"t":"Ischaemia","u":"glossary.html#ischaemia","k":"Glossary","s":"Not enough blood flow to part of the body.","b":""},{"t":"Kyphoplasty","u":"glossary.html#kyphoplasty","k":"Glossary","s":"Injecting bone cement into a broken vertebra after first making a space with a small balloon.","b":""},{"t":"Lamina","u":"glossary.html#lamina","k":"Glossary","s":"The flat plate of bone forming the back of each vertebra.","b":""},{"t":"Laminectomy","u":"glossary.html#laminectomy","k":"Glossary","s":"Removing part of the lamina to make more room for the nerves.","b":""},{"t":"Lesion","u":"glossary.html#lesion","k":"Glossary","s":"A general word for any area that looks different from normal. It does not mean cancer.","b":""},{"t":"Lumbar","u":"glossary.html#lumbar","k":"Glossary","s":"To do with the lower back. The lumbar spine has five vertebrae, L1 to L5.","b":""},{"t":"Lumbar puncture","u":"glossary.html#lumbar-puncture","k":"Glossary","s":"A test in which a thin needle is placed in the lower back to measure the pressure of the spinal fluid or take a sample.","b":""},{"t":"Malignant","u":"glossary.html#malignant","k":"Glossary","s":"Cancer. A malignant tumour can grow quickly and may spread.","b":""},{"t":"Meninges","u":"glossary.html#meninges","k":"Glossary","s":"The three layers that cover the brain and spinal cord: dura, arachnoid and pia.","b":""},{"t":"Meningioma","u":"glossary.html#meningioma","k":"Glossary","s":"A usually benign tumour that grows from the meninges.","b":""},{"t":"Metastasis","u":"glossary.html#metastasis","k":"Glossary","s":"A tumour that has spread from cancer elsewhere in the body.","b":""},{"t":"Midline shift","u":"glossary.html#midline-shift","k":"Glossary","s":"When swelling or a collection of blood pushes the brain across from its central position. It is a sign of significant pressure.","b":""},{"t":"MRI scan","u":"glossary.html#mri-scan","k":"Glossary","s":"Magnetic resonance imaging. A scan that uses a strong magnet and radio waves, with no X-rays, to show soft tissue in detail.","b":""},{"t":"Myelopathy","u":"glossary.html#myelopathy","k":"Glossary","s":"Damage to the spinal cord, often from pressure in the neck.","b":""},{"t":"Navigation","u":"glossary.html#navigation","k":"Glossary","s":"A system that links the patient's scan to their position during surgery, like GPS.","b":""},{"t":"Nerve root","u":"glossary.html#nerve-root","k":"Glossary","s":"The part of a spinal nerve as it leaves the spinal cord and the spine.","b":""},{"t":"Neuralgia","u":"glossary.html#neuralgia","k":"Glossary","s":"Sharp, often electric, pain along the path of a nerve.","b":""},{"t":"Occipital lobe","u":"glossary.html#occipital-lobe","k":"Glossary","s":"The back part of the brain. It handles vision.","b":""},{"t":"Oedema","u":"glossary.html#oedema","k":"Glossary","s":"Swelling caused by extra fluid. Around a tumour, it is often eased by steroid medicines.","b":""},{"t":"Parietal lobe","u":"glossary.html#parietal-lobe","k":"Glossary","s":"The upper back part of the brain. It handles touch, body position and spatial awareness.","b":""},{"t":"Pedicle","u":"glossary.html#pedicle","k":"Glossary","s":"The short, strong pillar of bone that joins the front and back of a vertebra. Fusion screws pass through it.","b":""},{"t":"Pituitary gland","u":"glossary.html#pituitary-gland","k":"Glossary","s":"A small gland below the brain that controls many of the body's hormones.","b":""},{"t":"Prolapse","u":"glossary.html#prolapse","k":"Glossary","s":"Another word for a slipped or herniated disc.","b":""},{"t":"Radiculopathy","u":"glossary.html#radiculopathy","k":"Glossary","s":"Pain, numbness or weakness caused by pressure on a nerve root. In the leg it is often called sciatica.","b":""},{"t":"Sciatica","u":"glossary.html#sciatica","k":"Glossary","s":"Pain that travels from the lower back or buttock down the leg, usually from pressure on a nerve root.","b":""},{"t":"Seizure","u":"glossary.html#seizure","k":"Glossary","s":"A sudden burst of abnormal electrical activity in the brain. It can cause jerking, staring or loss of awareness. Also called a fit.","b":""},{"t":"Shunt","u":"glossary.html#shunt","k":"Glossary","s":"A thin tube that drains extra fluid from the brain to another part of the body.","b":""},{"t":"Spinal cord","u":"glossary.html#spinal-cord","k":"Glossary","s":"The bundle of nerves running from the brainstem down the spine to about the level of the first lumbar vertebra.","b":""},{"t":"Spondylolisthesis","u":"glossary.html#spondylolisthesis","k":"Glossary","s":"When one vertebra slips forward on the one below.","b":""},{"t":"Spondylosis","u":"glossary.html#spondylosis","k":"Glossary","s":"Wear and tear of the spine with age.","b":""},{"t":"Stenosis","u":"glossary.html#stenosis","k":"Glossary","s":"Narrowing of a space, such as the spinal canal or a blood vessel.","b":""},{"t":"Stereotactic","u":"glossary.html#stereotactic","k":"Glossary","s":"Using precise three-dimensional coordinates from a scan to guide an instrument to a target.","b":""},{"t":"Subarachnoid haemorrhage","u":"glossary.html#subarachnoid-haemorrhage","k":"Glossary","s":"Bleeding into the space around the brain, often from a burst aneurysm.","b":""},{"t":"Subdural haematoma","u":"glossary.html#subdural-haematoma","k":"Glossary","s":"Blood collected between the dura and the surface of the brain.","b":""},{"t":"Syrinx","u":"glossary.html#syrinx","k":"Glossary","s":"A fluid-filled cavity inside the spinal cord.","b":""},{"t":"Temporal lobe","u":"glossary.html#temporal-lobe","k":"Glossary","s":"The part of the brain at the side, near the ears. It handles hearing, memory and, usually on the left, understanding language.","b":""},{"t":"Thoracic","u":"glossary.html#thoracic","k":"Glossary","s":"To do with the chest. The thoracic spine has twelve vertebrae, T1 to T12, each joined to a rib.","b":""},{"t":"Thrombectomy","u":"glossary.html#thrombectomy","k":"Glossary","s":"Removing a blood clot from an artery through a thin tube, as in some strokes.","b":""},{"t":"Ventricles","u":"glossary.html#ventricles","k":"Glossary","s":"Fluid-filled spaces inside the brain where cerebrospinal fluid is made and flows.","b":""},{"t":"Vertebra","u":"glossary.html#vertebra","k":"Glossary","s":"One of the 33 bones that make up the spine.","b":""},{"t":"Vertebroplasty","u":"glossary.html#vertebroplasty","k":"Glossary","s":"Injecting bone cement into a broken vertebra to steady it and ease pain.","b":""},{"t":"Thousands of years ago: Holes in the skull","u":"history.html","k":"History","s":"Skulls from many ancient cultures have neatly cut holes, a practice called trephination. Many show healed bone around the edge, which means people survived the operation. Why it was done is not certain. It may have been for head injuries, headaches or spiritual reasons.","b":""},{"t":"About 1600 BCE: The first written head injury cases","u":"history.html","k":"History","s":"The Edwin Smith Papyrus from ancient Egypt describes injuries to the head and spine in a calm, practical way. It is one of the earliest medical texts to mention the brain.","b":""},{"t":"About 1000 CE: Instruments drawn and described","u":"history.html","k":"History","s":"In Córdoba, the surgeon Al-Zahrawi wrote a large medical work that described and drew hundreds of surgical instruments, including tools for the skull.","b":""},{"t":"1664: The arteries of the brain","u":"history.html","k":"History","s":"Thomas Willis, an English doctor, published a detailed book on the brain. The ring of arteries at its base is still called the circle of Willis.","b":""},{"t":"1861: Speech has a home","u":"history.html","k":"History","s":"Paul Broca, a French doctor, showed that damage to a small area of the left frontal lobe takes away speech. It was strong early evidence that different parts of the brain do different jobs.","b":""},{"t":"1879 to 1884: Finding a tumour from symptoms","u":"history.html","k":"History","s":"William Macewen in Glasgow and, a few years later, Rickman Godlee in London removed brain tumours that had been located only from the patient's symptoms and examination. No scans existed yet.","b":""},{"t":"1895: X-rays","u":"history.html","k":"History","s":"Wilhelm Röntgen discovered X-rays in Germany on 8 November 1895. Within weeks, he had an image of the bones of his wife's hand. X-rays showed the skull, but not the brain.","b":""},{"t":"1900s to 1930s: Harvey Cushing makes surgery safer","u":"history.html","k":"History","s":"Harvey Cushing in the United States changed brain surgery from a very dangerous operation into a safer one. He recorded blood pressure during operations, used small silver clips to stop bleeding, and with William Bovie brought electrical cautery into the operating room.","b":""},{"t":"1918: Air outlines the brain","u":"history.html","k":"History","s":"Walter Dandy replaced the fluid inside the brain with air. On X-ray, the air showed the shape of the fluid spaces. If they were pushed aside, something was pressing on them.","b":""},{"t":"1927: The blood vessels appear","u":"history.html","k":"History","s":"Egas Moniz in Portugal injected dye into the neck arteries and took X-rays. For the first time, doctors could see the brain's blood vessels.","b":""},{"t":"1937: The first aneurysm clip","u":"history.html","k":"History","s":"Walter Dandy closed the neck of a brain aneurysm with a small silver clip. Clipping is still used today, with far better clips and microscopes.","b":""},{"t":"1947: Coordinates for the brain","u":"history.html","k":"History","s":"Ernest Spiegel and Henry Wycis described a frame that used three-dimensional coordinates to guide a needle to a deep target in the human brain. This is called stereotactic surgery.","b":""},{"t":"1950s: Shunts for hydrocephalus","u":"history.html","k":"History","s":"Reliable one-way valves made it possible to drain extra brain fluid into the body through a tube. Many children with hydrocephalus who would not have survived could now grow up.","b":""},{"t":"1957 to 1960s: The operating microscope","u":"history.html","k":"History","s":"Surgeons began operating with a microscope. Gazi Yaşargil in Zurich developed microneurosurgery in the 1960s, with fine instruments that made deep brain surgery much safer.","b":""},{"t":"1971: The first CT scan","u":"history.html","k":"History","s":"Godfrey Hounsfield's scanner made its first image of a patient's brain in London on 1 October 1971. It showed a tumour directly. Bleeding and strokes could now be seen in minutes.","b":""},{"t":"1977 to 1980s: MRI","u":"history.html","k":"History","s":"The first MRI scans of the human body were made in the late 1970s, and MRI entered hospitals in the 1980s. It shows the brain, spinal cord and nerves in fine detail, with no X-rays.","b":""},{"t":"1986: Navigation","u":"history.html","k":"History","s":"Frameless navigation linked the patient's scan to the surgeon's instruments, so the surgeon could see their exact position on the scan during the operation, like GPS.","b":""},{"t":"1987: Deep brain stimulation","u":"history.html","k":"History","s":"Alim-Louis Benabid in Grenoble showed that gentle electrical stimulation deep in the brain could stop a tremor. DBS is now used for Parkinson's disease and other movement disorders.","b":""},{"t":"1991: Coils for aneurysms","u":"history.html","k":"History","s":"Guido Guglielmi's detachable coils allowed aneurysms to be treated from inside the blood vessel, through a thin tube passed from the groin, with no opening in the skull.","b":""},{"t":"1990s to today: Mapping the wiring","u":"history.html","k":"History","s":"Functional MRI shows the areas used for speech and movement, and tractography shows the pathways that connect them. Endoscopes reach the pituitary through the nose. Some theatres can scan during the operation to check what has been removed.","b":""},{"t":"Neurosurgery","u":"neurosurgery.html","k":"Page","s":"Conditions treated in neurosurgery, explained in plain language.","b":""},{"t":"Spine","u":"spine.html","k":"Page","s":"Spine conditions and the surgical and non-surgical treatment options.","b":""},{"t":"About","u":"about.html","k":"Page","s":"About Dr Jemish Mayani and Insula Neurosciences.","b":""},{"t":"Contact","u":"contact.html","k":"Page","s":"How to get in touch for general, non-urgent questions.","b":""},{"t":"Medical disclaimer","u":"disclaimer.html","k":"Page","s":"The limits of the information on this website.","b":""},{"t":"Privacy policy","u":"privacy.html","k":"Page","s":"How this website handles information.","b":""}];
